[center][size=6][b]Smooth Midgar[/b][/size]
[size=4]Audio crackle and stutter fix for older CPUs, with no visual downgrade[/size][/center]

[line]

[size=5][b]Who this is for[/b][/size]
You're running [i]Final Fantasy VII Remake Intergrade[/i] on a CPU at or near the game's minimum spec, especially the [b]AMD FX family[/b] (FX-4300, FX-6300, FX-8320, FX-8350, FX-9590), and also Phenom II or early Intel quad-cores. Your graphics card handles high settings fine, but:

[list]
[*][b]The audio crackles, pops or turns to static[/b], and the usual fix ([font=Courier New]au.SetAudioChannelCount 0[/font]) mutes footsteps, fire and ambience.
[*][b]The game hitches[/b] in busy areas like the Sector 7 slums and Wall Market.
[/list]

Both problems come from the CPU. When an older processor is maxed out, the audio mixer misses its deadlines and asset loading arrives in bursts. This mod fixes the [b]scheduling[/b], not the graphics. [b]No visual setting is lowered.[/b] Play at the settings you already use.

[line]

[size=5][b]What it does[/b][/size]

[b]1. A tuned Engine.ini, generated for your PC[/b]
The installer detects your CPU threads, graphics memory and RAM, then writes a config sized to them:
[list]
[*]Larger, deeper audio buffers with a full voice budget: [b]no muted sounds[/b]
[*]Audio decode workers matched to your core count
[*]Texture and level streaming spread across frames, with the pool sized to your VRAM
[*]Dynamic resolution off, so the image stays sharp
[/list]

[b]2. A launcher that tunes Windows while you play[/b] (starts from Steam's Play button; everything reverts when the game closes)
[list]
[*]Temporary power plan: CPU held at full clock, all cores awake, PCIe and USB power saving off
[*]Starts the game with [font=Courier New]-USEALLAVAILABLECORES[/font]
[*]Game priority Above Normal
[*]Windows' audio engine priority High, if Steam runs as administrator (otherwise this one step is skipped; the Engine.ini audio fixes still apply)
[/list]

[line]

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/finalfantasy7remake/mods/74]FFVIIHook[/url] by emoose ([b]required[/b]; without it the game ignores Engine.ini)
[*]Windows 10 or 11
[*]Steam version (Epic works too; see below)
[/list]

[line]

[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Install [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/finalfantasy7remake/mods/74]FFVIIHook[/url]: put [font=Courier New]xinput1_3.dll[/font] next to [font=Courier New]ff7remake_.exe[/font] in [font=Courier New]End\Binaries\Win64[/font].
[*]Extract this mod anywhere, for example your desktop.
[*]Double-click [b]1 - Install (run once).bat[/b]. It shows what it detected, backs up your existing Engine.ini and writes the tuned one. Then it closes Steam for a moment, adds the launcher to the game's Steam launch options (your own options are kept), and restarts Steam.
[*]That's it: [b]press Play in Steam[/b] as usual. No extra window, no admin prompt.
[/list]

[b]Updating from 1.0.0:[/b] run the new [b]1 - Install (run once).bat[/b], then delete the old mod folder. The old "2 - Play FF7R.bat" is no longer used.

[b]Epic Games Store:[/b] double-click [b]Epic Games - Play FF7R.bat[/b], approve the admin prompt, then start the game from Epic and keep that window open while you play.

[b]What the launch option looks like:[/b] Steam runs [font=Courier New]powershell.exe ... -File "...\AppData\Local\SmoothMidgar\FF7R-Launcher.ps1" %command%[/font]. The launcher is plain PowerShell; open it in Notepad to see exactly what it does. It writes what it did to [font=Courier New]launcher.log[/font] in the same folder.

[line]

[size=5][b]Uninstall[/b][/size]
Double-click [b]2 - Uninstall.bat[/b]. It restores your original Engine.ini, removes the power plan, and takes the launcher out of the game's Steam launch options (your own options stay). FFVIIHook and your other mods are left alone.

[line]

[size=5][b]Fine-tuning[/b][/size]
Edit [font=Courier New]Documents\My Games\FINAL FANTASY VII REMAKE\Saved\Config\WindowsNoEditor\Engine.ini[/font]
[list]
[*][b]Sounds missing:[/b] raise [font=Courier New]AudioMaxChannels[/font] by 4 (max 48)
[*][b]Crackle remains:[/b] set [font=Courier New]AudioCallbackBufferFrameSize=3072[/font], or lower [font=Courier New]AudioMaxChannels[/font] by 4
[*][b]Textures load late:[/b] lower [font=Courier New]r.Streaming.PoolSize[/font] by 500
[/list]
Test audio live in the [b]~[/b] console: [font=Courier New]au.SetAudioChannelCount 28[/font]

[line]

[size=5][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Works with texture mods, ReShade, Special K and gameplay mods.
[*][b]Don't combine with other Engine.ini "optimization" or stutter-fix mods[/b]; they edit the same file. This mod already includes streaming fixes.
[*]Remove any [font=Courier New]au.SetAudioChannelCount 0[/font] line or console habit; it's no longer needed.
[/list]

[line]

[size=5][b]Tested on[/b][/size]
AMD FX-8350 / Radeon RX 6600 8 GB / 32 GB DDR3, max settings. Results on other FX-era systems are welcome in the Posts tab; please include your CPU, GPU and what the installer printed.

[line]

